        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe audit line is placed in the wrong unit. The three things it reports are all produced by the service's create path, not by the controller:\n\n- `ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner` (lines 252\u2013256) is what generates `registrationDate` (`rollToBusinessDay(...)`) and `customerCode` (`generateCustomerCode(...)`) \u2014 the exact two fields the audit line records.\n- That same method already has the create-only branch `if (owner.isNew()) { ... }` (line 257), which is the natural, existing home for a \"successful create\" side-effect. `owner.getId()` is likewise assigned by the `ownerRepository.save(owner)` call there.\n\nConsequences of logging in the controller instead:\n\n1. **Right-unit / altitude:** the audit statement is separated from the logic that computes the values it reports. The controller is a thin web adapter that only delegates to `saveOwner`; the \"owner created\" domain event actually happens in the service.\n2. **Reuse / duplication risk:** because the audit sits in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`, any other create path (a future `OwnerRestControllerV2.addOwner`, an importer, a batch job) that calls `saveOwner` will silently not emit the audit line, forcing this block to be copied. The service's `isNew()` branch is the single choke point that covers every create.\n\nSuggested change: move the `AUDIT` logger and the emission into `ClinicServiceImpl`, emitting it after `ownerRepository.save(owner)` gated on the creation case (capture `isNew()` before the save, since the id is assigned during save). That colocates the audit with the create logic and the fields it reports, and makes it fire once for all create callers. The controller's `addOwner` then stays a plain delegating handler.\n\nNote: do not simply drop it into `saveOwner` unguarded \u2014 that method also serves `updateOwner` (controller line 131), so the emission must stay inside the create/`isNew()` branch, not the shared path."
